Verified against `origin/main` (06e187c) in this session; the mechanism and its consequence were reproduced by execution, twice independently, in throwaway worktrees. The data loss is **live today**; the destructive undo is latent behind an undo affordance that does not exist yet.

## What is VERIFIED

**1. A gesture sits in an unpersisted buffer until the *next* gesture arrives.**

```ts
// src/simulation/construction/system.ts:65-75
public registerTransactionOrder(orderId: string, transactionId?: string): void {
  if (transactionId !== this.currentTransactionId) {
    if (this.currentTransaction.length > 0) {
      this.undoStack.push([...this.currentTransaction]);
    }
    this.currentTransaction = [];
    this.currentTransactionId = transactionId;
    this.redoStack = []; // Clear redo stack on new action
  }
  this.currentTransaction.push(orderId);
}
```

There is no tick-based or time-based flush. The only other flush point is `undo()` itself, which proves the intended semantics — the pending gesture *is* the top of the undo history:

```ts
// src/simulation/construction/system.ts:77-82
public undo(): void {
  if (this.currentTransaction.length > 0) {
    this.undoStack.push([...this.currentTransaction]);
    this.currentTransaction = [];
    this.currentTransactionId = undefined;
  }
```

**2. The save carries neither field, and restore blanks them.**

```ts
// src/simulation/construction/system.ts:324-328
return {
  orders,
  undoStack: this.undoStack.map((transaction) => [...transaction]),
  redoStack: this.redoStack.map((transaction) => [...transaction]),
};
```

```ts
// src/simulation/construction/system.ts:336-339
this.undoStack = data.undoStack.map((transaction) => [...transaction]);
this.redoStack = data.redoStack.map((transaction) => [...transaction]);
this.currentTransaction = [];
this.currentTransactionId = undefined;
```

The `ConstructionSnapshot` interface (`system.ts:8-12`) has no field for either.

**3. It is a wrong-target undo, which is worse than a no-op.** Reproduced twice: gesture A (`a1,a2` / `tx-a`) then gesture B (`b1,b2,b3` / `tx-b`). `snapshot().undoStack` is `[["a1","a2"]]` — B absent — while `snapshot().orders` holds all five. Control, no save: undo cancels B and leaves A. After snapshot → restore → undo: **A is cancelled and B is left standing.** A second undo does nothing (the stack is exhausted), and no later gesture re-adds B, so B's orders sit in `data.orders` reachable by neither undo nor redo for the rest of that save lineage. With only one gesture ever placed, the same undo is a total no-op.

**4. The contract is promised in code, not merely implied.**

```ts
// src/simulation/runtime/restore-session.ts:104-109
export const CURRENT_SAVE_RESTORED_SCOPE: RestoredScope = {
  restored: [
    ...
    'construction orders and undo/redo',
```

`restoreSimulationRuntime` returns this "so a caller -- and the player-facing UI -- can be honest about what came back" (`restore-session.ts:94-95`), it is pinned by `tests/determinism/snapshot-restore-fidelity.test.ts:214`, and `docs/PERSISTENCE.md` repeats it.

## Corrections to the original claim

- **Not a race.** The audit blamed an autosave landing mid-gesture. All segments of one gesture are submitted synchronously from `src/ui/build-tool.ts:102-117` with one projected `executeAtTick`, and `Kernel.step()` drains all commands due at a tick without yielding (`src/simulation/kernel/kernel.ts:104-114`), so the gesture *is* atomic with respect to capture. The bug needs no race: the buffer is simply always non-empty after any gesture.
- **Not the interval autosave.** `SessionController.markDirty()` (`session-controller.ts:178-181`) has no caller anywhere in `src/`. The real triggers are stronger — the deliberate "Save now" button (`src/ui/save-panel.ts` → `controller.saveNow`) and `visibilitychange`/`pagehide` via `LifecycleSaveHandler` (`src/main.ts:359-367`), both reaching `host.capture()`.

## LIVE for the data loss, LATENT for the destructive undo

Every save taken after a build gesture omits that gesture from the undo history — unconditionally, today, on native V3 saves. So corrupt saves are being written now; the latency is in the consumer.

What keeps the wrong-target cancel out of players' hands is that nothing emits the command: `{ type: 'Undo' }` exists only in the protocol (`src/simulation/protocol/commands.ts:47`) and the handler (`src/simulation/construction/handler.ts:42-43`); grep over `src/ui`, `src/main.ts`, `src/rendering` and `src/input` finds only prose comments. The day an undo button or keybinding is wired — which `src/ui/build-tool.ts:16-22` explicitly plans for ("a twelve-segment wall undoes as one wall rather than as twelve taps on undo") — the wrong-target cancel goes live with no further change.

## Smallest fix

Persist the buffer; do **not** flush it at capture time.

1. Add two optional fields to `ConstructionSnapshot` (`system.ts:8-12`) — `currentTransaction?: readonly string[]` and `currentTransactionId?: string` — emit them in `snapshot()` (:324-328), and read them in `restore()` in place of the unconditional reset at :338-339.
2. Add the matching optional keys to `constructionSnapshotSchema` (`src/persistence/save-schema.ts:148-154`). **Required**, because the object is `.strict()` and is shared by the V1 (:680), V2 (:689) and V3 (:708) payload shapes — a hand-verified probe confirms that without it `decodeSaveEnvelope` returns `{ok:false, error:{code:'invalid-shape', message:'... payload.construction: Unrecognized key: "currentTransaction"'}}`.

No save-version bump and no migration step. This is exactly the `edge` precedent, whose in-place justification at `save-schema.ts:118-129` applies verbatim — "Optional, and **not** a version bump… Because it is optional it is equally valid in the V1 and V2 payload shapes above… so no migration step is needed and none is added." A pre-fix save simply has no pending transaction, which is today's behaviour. Write that reasoning into the schema comment as `edge` did; that is the repo's convention for a no-bump additive change.

**Do NOT "fix" this by flushing `currentTransaction` inside `snapshot()`.** Capture is contractually read-only ("Reads only the runtime's own snapshot methods", `restore-session.ts:158-162`), and committing the open gesture at save time would make *saving* change the live session's undo granularity — a save-observable behaviour change.

### One decision, and it is already closed by an existing pin

The two shapes are *resume* (persist both fields; a further segment with the same transaction id rejoins the gesture) and *commit* (persist only the order list and have `restore()` push it onto `undoStack`). **The commit variant fails an existing pin** — `tests/determinism/snapshot-restore-fidelity.test.ts:169` (`expect(toJsonValue(afterFirst.construction)).toEqual(toJsonValue(bundle.construction))`) and its fixed-point assertion, because the restored session would report the gesture as a committed `undoStack` entry while the source bundle reported it pending. So ship **resume**, and record the choice in the schema comment rather than blocking on an ADR. No ADR governs undo/transaction semantics today (0001–0016, plus 0018 on #91, are silent).

## Test that should have caught it

Two tests are blind by construction.

- `tests/unit/undo-redo.test.ts` is the only undo/redo test and never saves — it drives place/undo/redo through the kernel in one session, exercising the flush at `system.ts:78-82` and never the persistence boundary.
- `tests/determinism/snapshot-restore-fidelity.test.ts` and `tests/helpers/determinism-state.ts:80` compare sessions *through* `runtime.construction.snapshot()` — the very surface that omits `currentTransaction` — so any state the snapshot cannot see is equal on both sides by definition. Verified: the restored and live snapshots are identical *before* the undo and diverge only when `undo()` runs.

No test in the repository ever asserts a non-empty `undoStack` crossing a save; every `undoStack`/`redoStack` literal in `tests/` is `[]`.

**Surviving mutation, run not reasoned:** replace `system.ts:326-327` with `undoStack: [], redoStack: [],` — i.e. make the save carry no undo history at all — and the suite still passes (132 passed across `tests/determinism` + `tests/integration` + `tests/migrations` + `undo-redo` + `construction-geometry`; full-suite 1161 passed, the one failure being an unrelated load-dependent timeout in `tests/unit/prisoners-operations-scenario.test.ts`).

**The missing test is behavioural, not structural:** place gesture A, place gesture B, capture, restore, `undo()`, and assert B's orders are `cancelled` and A's are not.

## Open PRs touching the same files

**#91 (`claude/construction-materials-supply`) and #97 (`claude/materials-sync-validation-ph2edu`) both touch `src/simulation/construction/system.ts`**, in a different hunk: they replace `// TODO: release materials` in `cancelOrder` (main :156) with a `materialsProvider.release(...)` refund. That shifts the lines a fix must edit — on those branches `snapshot()` is at **:323-337** and `restore()`'s reset at **:346-347**, not main's :315-329 / :338-339. A fix touching the field declarations (:32-38), `snapshot()` and `restore()` should still merge cleanly. Neither #91 nor #94 touches `src/persistence/save-schema.ts` or `src/simulation/runtime/restore-session.ts`.

Semantically **#91 makes this worse**: after the round trip the wrong-target undo now also refunds the *wrong* orders' materials into the construction container while the orders the player wanted removed keep theirs, so the defect becomes inventory-visible as well as geometry-visible.

## Adjacent quirk, noted not filed

A `PlaceBuildOrder` with `transactionId` omitted after an `undo()` matches the `undefined` `currentTransactionId` at `system.ts:66`, so it appends to the buffer *without* clearing `redoStack`, leaving a live redo entry for orders the player has moved past. Unreachable from `BuildTool`, which always generates a transaction id (`build-tool.ts:106`).

## Related

- **This issue and its companion test-blindness issue are two halves of one area.** This one is the *behaviour*: the newest gesture is never in the save, and the first undo after a load therefore cancels the wrong gesture. The companion is *why no test caught it*: the determinism-fidelity harness compares two sessions through `runtime.construction.snapshot()`, the very surface that omits the field, so the omission is invisible to the suite by construction. Fixing the behaviour without fixing the harness leaves the next omission of the same kind equally undetectable — the two should be read, and ideally closed, together. (Cross-reference added below once the companion was filed.)
- **Collision with open PR #97** (`claude/materials-sync-validation-ph2edu`), which also touches `src/simulation/construction/system.ts` — it adds the materials refund in `cancelOrder`. See the "Open PRs touching the same files" section above for the exact line shifts a fix must expect on that branch, and for the way #97/#91 make the wrong-target undo inventory-visible as well as geometry-visible. Land order matters here: whoever goes second rebases onto shifted line numbers in the same file.
